<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="authentication"
      updated="July 31, 2026"
      source_path="portal/apps/emisar_web/lib/emisar_web/controllers/marketing_html/docs_authentication.html.heex"
      toc={[
        {"sign-in", "Choose a sign-in method"},
        {"enforcement", "Enforce account sign-in"},
        {"provisioning", "Provision and remove members"},
        {"providers", "Provider support"},
        {"sessions", "Sessions and offboarding"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="authentication">
        <:dek>
          Choose how people sign in, how the account enforces that choice, and whether your
          directory or an emisar administrator owns the member lifecycle.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Authentication proves who the person is. Their emisar role and runner access decide what
        they can do and which hosts they can reach. Keep those as separate decisions: changing the
        sign-in method does not widen a member's infrastructure access.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sign-in">Choose a sign-in method</.docs_h2>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Method</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">How it works</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Use it when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Magic link</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                An expiring email code is paired with a nonce held by the browser that started the
                sign-in. The code is single-use.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                You do not need organization-managed sign-in, or you are testing account access
                before enforcing SSO.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">OIDC SSO</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                A confidential OpenID Connect client redirects the member to your identity
                provider, then binds the returning identity to its stable provider subject.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Your identity provider should own authentication and its MFA policy. Available on
                Team and Enterprise.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Read
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access#sessions"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Sessions &amp; credentials</.link>
        for the magic-link and session model. To connect an IdP, continue with <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          Single sign-on
        </.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="enforcement">Enforce account sign-in</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require MFA</strong>
            routes members who have not enrolled TOTP to setup before they can use the account. An
            SSO connection can satisfy the requirement only when you mark that provider as
            enforcing MFA; otherwise emisar still asks for TOTP.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require SSO</strong>
            accepts only an SSO identity issued for this account. Members who arrived through a
            magic link are sent back through the account's provider on their next navigation.
            emisar requires an enabled connection before you turn it on and refuses to disable or
            delete the last enabled connection while it remains required.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Be precise about what Require SSO covers, because it is a control on <em>people signing in
        with a browser</em>, not on credentials that already exist. It gates the account's console
        pages, the audit CSV download, and the OAuth consent screen that mints a client key — every
        member, including owners and admins, with no bypass. It does not authenticate an MCP bearer
        token, the audit export API, SCIM, an OAuth token or refresh exchange, or an OAuth access
        token that was already issued. Turning it on revokes no existing API key and no existing
        OAuth grant: bounce the sessions you want gone and revoke the credentials you want gone,
        through <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >rotate and revoke credentials</.link>. The requirement applies only while at least one
        provider is enabled — which is why emisar refuses to remove the last one — and a provider
        that is enabled but broken or unreachable does not open the gate; sign-in simply fails until
        it works.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Complete a real SSO sign-in before requiring it">
        <p>
          Test connection proves discovery and endpoint reachability; it does not prove your client
          assignment, redirect URI, claims, or login policy. Keep an owner session open, sign in
          through the provider in another browser, then turn on Require SSO from <a href={
            ~p"/app/team"
          }>Team → Security</a>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="provisioning">Provision and remove members</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Pick one system to own the lifecycle. The sign-in protocol and the lifecycle protocol are
        separate: OIDC authenticates a person; SCIM creates, updates, suspends, and groups them.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Invitations</strong>
            let an owner or admin create the membership and choose its role before the person signs
            in. This is the direct emisar-managed path.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO auto-provisioning</strong>
            creates a member on first successful sign-in at the connection's default role and
            runner access. Manual approval holds that first sign-in for an administrator instead.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM directory sync</strong>
            provisions the membership before first login, applies group mappings, and suspends it
            when the directory marks the user inactive. SCIM is the path when offboarding in your
            IdP must revoke emisar access automatically.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Teams &amp; access
        </.link>
        owns roles, runner scopes, invitations, and manual suspension. The
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          SCIM guide
        </.link>
        owns the directory lifecycle and protocol contract.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider support</.docs_h2>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Provider</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Identity claim</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Directory sync</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >
                  Okta
                </.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>sub</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Separate SCIM application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >
                  Microsoft Entra
                </.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>oid</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Separate enterprise application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >JumpCloud</.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>sub</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Same custom application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Google Workspace</.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>sub</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">No outbound SCIM for a custom app</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Keycloak</.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>sub</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Needs a third-party outbound SCIM client</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/sso#generic-oidc"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >
                  Generic OpenID Connect
                </.link>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>sub</code></td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Any client that implements the documented SCIM subset</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="sessions">Sessions and offboarding</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Members can inspect and revoke their active sessions. An administrator can suspend a
            membership, which ends that account's sessions and revokes API keys minted from it.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Disabling or deleting an SSO connection blocks new sign-ins and revokes sessions
            authenticated through identities on that connection. It does not delete the users or
            their audit history.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            SCIM deprovisioning suspends rather than deletes. Reactivation can restore a directory
            suspension, while a manual administrator suspension stays under administrator control.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Offboarding a person ends what that membership issued. It does not touch account-level
        credentials someone else still holds — enrollment keys, audit-export tokens, the SCIM
        bearer, signing material. Rotating those is <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >rotate and revoke credentials</.link>; doing it under pressure, in the order that preserves
        evidence, is <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security incidents</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If sign-in fails, start with the symptom table in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          SSO troubleshooting
        </.link>. If provisioning or group access is stale, use
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          SCIM troubleshooting
        </.link>
        and check the connection's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Last sync</strong>
        signal first. Both are reachable from the identity section of <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting" <> "#identity"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >troubleshooting</.link>, which is where to start when you're not yet sure the problem is
        identity at all.
        For an account lockout with no working owner session, email <a
          href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.support()}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >support</a>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
